    {
      "id": 55,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        1200,
        1800
      ],
      "size": [
        1200,
        90
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 9,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "These are the nodes for the HighresFix setup. The Flux Highres Fix Scaler provides functionality for injecting noise. For motifs with a high-key background or particularly busy scenes, you can adjust this parameter as needed. If you notice excessive moles or facial hair, reduce the noise scale. The initial setup generally works well out of the box. In 90% of cases, the images will not exhibit the infamous flux checkerboard pattern. If this issue does occur, increase the noise level.\n\nYou can also experiment with steps and denoising in the final image. The Model Sampling Flux Normalized node functions similarly to the standard Model Sampling Flux node. For upscaling, I typically recommend using a higher max shift. Additionally, you can adjust steps, denoising, and Multiply Sigmas to address particularly persistent checkerboarding. However, keep in mind that the AYS scheduler burns in quickly when using flux."
      ],
      "color": "#2a363b",
      "bgcolor": "#3f5159"
    },
    {
      "id": 56,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        -390,
        1580
      ],
      "size": [
        590,
        310
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 12,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "ABOUT",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "This workflow lets you perform inference at higher resolutions than the standard Flux Dev model typically allows. High-resolution inference with the standard base model often introduces checkerboarding artifacts in images and produces issues with coherent noise scheduling. If you haven’t encountered these problems yourself, you can find examples in my Civitai post. https://civitai.com/user/42lux/articles\n\nTo address these challenges, there are several QoL custom nodes designed for higher resolution workflows. The main one is the Model Sampling Flux Normalized node, which normalizes the sigma schedule to support higher resolutions effectively. An empty latent picker with optimized resolution sizes. The Flux Highres Fix Scaler for automatic scaling with noise injection and a textbox with integrated token counter.\n\nFor optimal results, I try to keep my CLIP-L tokens under 77 and my T5-XXL tokens under 256, although 512 also works reasonably well. Beyond these limits, you may observe unwanted results, with this inference setup the model gets a bit more literal and likes longer prompts in the T5-XXL. \n\nThe sampler setup is quite delicate, so I recommend adjusting it only if you know what you are doing, particularly when working with the high-resolution fix sampler and scheduler.\n\nThis workflow is designed to work in conjunction with my UltimateAtHome LoRa. The LoRa’s primary purpose is to resolve the noise scheduling issues encountered with Flux Dev when performing inference above 1.5 MP. \n\nHave fun experimenting\n\n- 42lux"
      ],
      "color": "#2a363b",
      "bgcolor": "#3f5159"
    },
    {
      "id": 57,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        210,
        1580
      ],
      "size": [
        480,
        310
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 7,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "SETTINGS",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "UltimateAtHome LoRa:\nThe LoRa model tends towards realism. For general use, a strength of around 0.25 is recommended, as this primarily addresses the noise schedule in high-resolution sampling. In the range of 0.5–1, the LoRa also corrects issues with flux skin and chin artifacts.\n\nModelSamplingFluxNormalized and CLIPTextEncodeFlux:\nModelSamplingFlux and guidance behave similarly to the normal flux version.\nRecommended guidance values range between 2.5–4.2.\n\nPAG Attention:\nHigher PAG values enhance overall detail. A good general value is 1.75, though personally, I would not recommend exceeding 3.\n\nLyingSigmaSampler:\nHigher values refine smaller local details. I suggest not exceeding -0.1.\n\nFluxHighresFixScaler:\nThis setting scales and introduces noise, allowing you to choose the desired upscale method and the amount of noise to apply. If you notice residual noise in the high-resolution fix (e.g., blotchy or uneven backgrounds), this is the parameter to adjust.\n\nMultiplySigmas:\nThis functions similarly to the Lying Sigma Sampler but for the highresfix inference."
      ],
      "color": "#2a363b",
      "bgcolor": "#3f5159"
    },
    {
      "id": 58,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        700,
        1580
      ],
      "size": [
        360,
        310
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 8,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "HARDWARE REQS AND PERFORMANCE",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "HARDWARE REQS:\n\nThe workflow just barely fits into 16GB of VRAM and 32GB System RAM at 6MP resolution.\n\nFor Ada Lovelace architecture cards, like the 4xxxx series, it’s best to use “fp8_e4m3fn_fast” along with Torch Compile. However, since many people encounter issues with Torch Compile, it’s not included in this basic workflow.\n\nFor Ampere Architecture cards, like the 3xxxx series, using GGUF quants is recommended; otherwise, you will consistently run out of memory (OOM).\n\nPERFORMANCE:\n\n4090 w/o compile:\n3MP =  85s\n6MP =  220s\n\n4090 w compile:\n3MP = 30s. \n6MP = 95s"
      ],
      "color": "#2a363b",
      "bgcolor": "#3f5159"
    },
